    {
      "id": "caching",
      "title": "Saved input has its own rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Prompt caching lets a provider reuse earlier processing for matching input. There may be a charge to write that material before later discounted reads. Entries can expire, and changed text near the beginning can prevent reuse. The calculator assumes the preceding input stays eligible; it does not promise that a real service will deliver those cache hits.",
          "sources": [
            "openaicache",
            "anthropiccache"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The selected OpenAI rows use writes at 1.25 times ordinary input and reads at one tenth. Claude uses five-minute writes at 1.25 times input; Fable 5.1 has a different read discount from Opus 5 and Sonnet 5. Grok and DeepSeek charge ordinary input for misses on these rate cards, with no separately listed write premium.",
          "sources": [
            "openai",
            "anthropic",
            "grok",
            "deepseek"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Cached input still occupies the context window: the maximum material the model can handle at once. The calculator suppresses totals if a step exceeds its selected limit. It also checks each whole prompt for higher rates: above 272,000 input tokens for these OpenAI models, and at or above 200,000 for Grok 4.6.",
          "sources": [
            "openai",
            "grok"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "aliases": []
    },
