      "title": "Spend on useful progress",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "A low token price only helps if the job finishes acceptably. Try representative tasks, check the results and count repeated attempts. A cheaper model that needs more retries may cost more per successful outcome. A more expensive model is not automatically worth its price either.",
          "sources": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Define what a finished, acceptable result looks like.",
            "Provide relevant reference material and concise tool results.",
            "Set a spending or stopping limit for repeated attempts.",
            "Reuse stable instructions when the service supports it.",
            "Compare total cost for work that passes the same checks."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Long jobs may use compaction, which summarizes earlier material, or a fresh session with a written handoff. Both can reduce carried context, but can lose details and require extra work. Anthropic’s harness experiments discuss both approaches; they do not establish that resets are always necessary or always cheaper.",
          "sources": [
            "harness"
          ]
        }
